From: kiritakekumi <pujingyu@iscas.ac.cn>
Date: Tue, 9 Sep 2026 20:22:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] RISC-V: fix CTZ reuse in bclr_lowest_set_bit

A rotated set-bit iteration loop can put a CTZ immediately after an
in-place x &= x - 1.  Reusing that CTZ clears another bit because it
observes the updated input.  Check the search boundary instructions as
well as the intervening instructions, and reject CTZ output overlaps
that make the later-CTZ transformation unsafe.

Also preserve operation widths when matching the idiom.  A sign-extended
32-bit decrement is not generally a 64-bit decrement, and a CTZ of the
low 32 bits cannot locate a set bit in the upper half of a 64-bit value.
Require both operations to cover all observable result bits.  Preserve
valid RV64 word optimizations when dataflow proves that only the low
32 bits are used, and remove stale equality notes after replacement.

Add regression tests for loop iteration, both width mismatches, and
width boundaries in the existing positive optimization cases.

Validated with a rebuilt GCC 17 cc1: 40 runtime configurations,
1,052,160 differential comparisons, 15 assembly configurations and
7 -fcompare-debug checks.  Full bootstrap/DejaGNU testing was not run.

This is a standalone combined patch; it supersedes and replaces
2002-riscv-fix-bclr-sign-extension.patch, whose sign extension case is
handled here by refusing the transformation instead.
Base-commit: fe89c51bc6b94fd6aa95f5ffbabc6a0f40274798

Co-authored-by: Jiawei Chen <jiawei@iscas.ac.cn>
Co-authored-by: Jingyu Pu <pujingyu@iscas.ac.cn>
Signed-off-by: Jingyu Pu <pujingyu@iscas.ac.cn>
---
